<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christmas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Christmas Day, Xmas, Noel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christmas is a religious and cultural celebration commemorating the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a>, observed primarily on 25 December; part of the Eastern Christian churches keep it on 25 December of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>, which currently corresponds to 7 January. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> of Luke and Matthew describe Jesus as born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. The earliest document to place the birth on 25 December is the Chronograph of 354, in a part generally agreed to have been written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 336; the day was the traditional date of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a> prohibited the celebration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> from 1647 to 1660. Its customs mix pre-Christian, Christian and secular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christmas.jpg" alt="Christmas" width="330" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bartolomé Esteban Murillo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adoration_of_the_Shepherds,_Murillo_(Prado_Museum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince Levon II, who acceded in 1187, changed that: with the support of the Holy Roman Emperors Frederick Barbarossa and Henry VI he was crowned in the cathedral of Tarsus on 6 January 1198, the Armenian <b>Christmas</b>, by the Catholicos Gregory VI Abirad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On <b>Christmas</b> Day 800 CE, Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne in St. Peter's Basilica in Rome, where he was acclaimed Imperator Romanorum, making him the first emperor in the West since the deposition of Romulus Augustulus in 476 CE and establishing the ideal of a unified Christian Europe…</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After victories over the Poles, Bohdan Khmelnytsky entered Kyiv at <b>Christmas</b> 1648, hailed as a liberator from Polish captivity, and in February 1649 told a Polish delegation at Pereiaslav that he meant to be hetman of a Ruthenia stretching to Chelm and Halych.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Holy Roman Empire traced its origin to the coronation of Charlemagne by Pope Leo III in Rome on <b>Christmas</b> Day 800 CE, an act that deliberately invoked the legacy of the western Roman Empire to legitimise Frankish dominance over Latin Christendom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He was crowned king at <b>Christmas</b> 1000 or on 1 January 1001, with a crown that, according to his later legends, was sent by Pope Sylvester II with the approval of Emperor Otto III.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Godfrey died in July 1100, his brother Baldwin, count of Edessa, came south and was crowned King Baldwin I at Bethlehem on <b>Christmas</b> Day 1100.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
